    "['set policy-options policy-statement [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'router bgp 100', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper `set protocols bgp group internal export EXPORT-LOOPBACK` command to Cisco IOS configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 10.2.1.1 route-map EXPORT-LOOPBACK out\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS, you would need additional configuration for the route-map EXPORT-LOOPBACK to match the equivalent functionality of the Juniper policy-statement. The basic structure shown above is how you apply a route-map (equivalent to Juniper's policy-statement) to BGP neighbor advertisements in Cisco.\", 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 10.2.1.1 route-map EXPORT-LOOPBACK out', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'neighbor 10.2.1.1 route-map EXPORT-LOOPBACK out', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper `set protocols bgp group internal export EXPORT-LOOPBACK` command to Cisco IOS configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 10.2.1.1 route-map EXPORT-LOOPBACK out\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS, you would need additional configuration for the route-map EXPORT-LOOPBACK to match the equivalent functionality of the Juniper policy-statement. The basic structure shown above is how you apply a route-map (equivalent to Juniper's policy-statement) to BGP neighbor advertisements in Cisco.\", 'trans_template': 'neighbor 10.2.1.1 route-map EXPORT-LOOPBACK out'}]]": 1,
